<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Virunum</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Virunum was the capital of the Roman province of Noricum, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zollfeld">Zollfeld</a> plain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. It was founded under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> around 45 CE to replace the hilltop settlement on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdalensberg">Magdalensberg</a>, and had a forum, temples and an amphitheatre. It declined in the late Roman period and was abandoned during the migrations of the 5th and 6th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/virunum.jpg" alt="Virunum" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Jaritz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maria_Saal_Zollfeld_Virunum_Arena_Suedteil_22092006_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town's full name was Municipium Claudium Virunum. It may have taken its name from the hilltop settlement on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdalensberg">Magdalensberg</a>, widely believed to have been the royal capital of the pre-Roman Celtic kingdom of Noricum. It stood on a flood-proof terrace on the main route from the Adriatic to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, with a branch to the Amber Road, and its territory covered about 9,000 square kilometres of central and lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>. The city had the Latin Right, and its council, magistrates and dual mayors are known in part by name.</p><p>Virunum was the seat of the provincial governor until the middle of the 2nd century. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a> the administration moved to Ovilava, now Wels, though the province's finances stayed in Virunum, and when Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> divided Noricum it became the capital of Noricum mediterraneum. It was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s see from 343. Unfortified and on a flat valley floor, the city was probably partly or wholly left by its people for nearby hills such as the Ulrichsberg, and in the 5th century Teurnia is named as the capital of Noricum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The city covered about one square kilometre on a checkerboard plan. Digging began in the 18th century, with systematic excavations from the end of the 19th century to 1931 and again from the end of the 20th, which uncovered the amphitheatre. Finds include the forum and Capitolium, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> mosaic of almost 30 square metres, a sanctuary of Jupiter Dolichenus, inscriptions from two Mithraea, votive plates from a temple of Nemesis and an early Christian church. On the slope of Töltschach Hill stood an amphitheatre and the only Roman theatre with a stage known in Noricum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Roman stones from Virunum are built into the Prunnerkreuz, a small shrine of 1692 put up by Johann Dominikus Prunner, who believed the town had been called Sala. Virunum is today a Catholic titular see, and in summer its amphitheatre is used for historical re-enactments with the Carinthian State Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Virunum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Membership lists, such as the graffiti of Dura-Europos and the bronze album of <b>Virunum</b>, name only men, and Gordon concludes that the cult was for men only, though Porphyry refers to female initiates and David Jonathan has suggested that women took part in some places.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mithraism">Mithraism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 16 BCE Noricum was absorbed peacefully into the Roman Empire, becoming the province of Noricum with towns such as <b>Virunum</b> and Celeia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-noricum">Kingdom of Noricum</a></li></ul></section>
